A||||14||MooCow||Are Fiero's good cars??||||5
Z||000000||MooCow||11-19-1999||09:01 AM||||My neighbor is a car freak and is always working on cars.  When I told him I wanted to buy a Fiero he said you better have a lot of money saved up for repaires.  Is this true or what? I really want a fiero but I'm only 17 with only $3500 to $4000 for my next car.  I really want a fun, small car like a Fiero but don't feel like paying an arm and a leg on repaires.  All info on this matter will help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-19-1999||09:17 AM||||You could probably find a decent Fiero for about $1600(US).  With the rest of the money you could get it fixed up really nice.  Plan on doing a lot of your own repairs.   That will save a ton of money for you.  If you ever have questions or need help,  the forum is here.  And there are a lot of people with a lot of Fiero knowledge on it!  I say GO FOR IT!!  Join the ranks of some the most proud cars owners ever!  Become a Fierofile!!!  Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||ggarsk||11-19-1999||09:26 AM||||If your get a Fiero that has been taken care of the repair bills will be less. Just about any used car will cost you in repairs but none are as fun to drive as a Fiero. Go for it,you won't regret it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||dogtired||11-19-1999||10:03 AM||||I have often wanted to reply to posts similar to this such as:<BR>Is this car worth XX$$ amount of dollars?<BR>Is this car rare? Why or Why not?<BR>Will I get my moneys Worth?<BR>and then What about maintenance?<BR>My first comment has to be that beauty is in the eye of the beer holder.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> or the drunker you get the better the gals at the bar look!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Whichever, it is a relative notion.<BR>If you want grandma's Vega or Chevette that only has 50k on it, you could get some relative cheep miles out of them. But if you drive the same car like it were a Fiero or Vette you will end up putting a lot of maintenance money in to it. So If you take a Fiero with 100k on it and drive it like you are on a limited budget you will probably not have quite the repair bills some of us have?<BR>Mechanics seem to cuss Fiero's because there isn't enough room to cuss down beside the motor. But you take just about any other vehicle to that same mechanic and they will say the same thing? enough rambling.<BR>You have to make the choice (I can only try to influence that) Do you want to have a car with a lot of CLASS or are you looking just to save money? There are a lot of options and variables here. I currently own several cars that I can't aford (so if you have any of that $4000 left over send it here) but I feel it necessary to drive something better than grandma's Buick.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I have an 86GT and 87SE both V6's and I find as a seasoned shade tree mechanic, they really aren't bad to work on. The price for parts isn't bad sometimes you need to shop around. With the amount of money you are talking (in my OPINION) you could purchase a really nice specimen that needed nothing more than being driven or you could buy a fixer-upper for about half (or less) that amount and budget the other half into what needs repaired and when?<BR>Just my Opinion! Man, can you tell I have been up all night or what?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Don't forget Beauty is in the eye of the BEER holder!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||PAUL88GT||11-19-1999||01:01 PM||||Most any 84-88 vehicle is going to require maintenance.  My advice is if you are going to buy a Fiero, you should plan on doing some of the maintenance yourself to save money.  In general the parts are less than parts for Japanese imports, but you have to shop around. This includes making trips to junkyards. Most mechanics will not shop around because they pass on the price of parts directly to you. If you are not mechanically inclined, get a newer model vehicle.  Fieros reqire some investment of "sweat equity" unless you have money to burn.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott2M4||11-19-1999||01:29 PM||||I got a similar ear full about two months ago when I started shopping for my latest toy.  "Money pits", "hard to work on", "requires constant care", and I can't tell you how many times I heard "wasn't that the car that caught fire?"!!!!!<P>Money pit?  Maybe, but won't anything that's 12-16 years old potentially be a money pit?  Hard to work on?  Maybe some things, but I just put a new belt, tensioner, valve cover gasket and alternator on mine and I've never put a tool on a car before (except to change the oil).  Requires constant care?  Depends on the care that was given to it prior to the purchase.  But, I wanted something to tinker with on the weekends.  Engine fires?  Corrected during the first year with a recall.<P>RZURGTN and PAUL88GT are correct.  It helps to do some/most/all of the repair work yourself.  Examples:  My mechanic wanted $100 to replace the serpentine belt.  I did it for the cost of the part - $23.  The same mechanic wanted $75 to replace the VC gasket.  I did it for $7.  Most important thing to mention here is the fact that the people on this forum encouraged me to do it myself, they provided great information on how to do it myself, and they were there if I was having trouble.<P>I looked at CRXs, MR2s, 300ZXs and RX-7s - all nice cars.  But, consider the fact that, on average, the price of an import part will be double that of the American counterpart.  Plus, the Fiero was a parts-bin car.  Many of the components were used on other GM models - making parts easier to find and cheaper.  Finally, how many 12-16 year old competing models can you find that don't have to be concerned with body rust (thank you, Pontiac!)?<P>You will not find a better group of people than here on this forum.  I'm glad I went the route of the Fiero ... you will be too!<p>[This message has been edited by Scott2M4 (edited 11-19-1999).]||||reg||4
Z||000006||Richard Parnell||11-19-1999||01:55 PM||||I bought my 87 SC for $1000 and have put about that much into it over a 3 year period. I drive it to work every day and have a nice CD in it for sounds.  Pretty good value I'd say, plus it's sooo much fun to drive.  If you enjoy driving/cruising go for it.||||reg||12
Z||000007||Shiner||11-19-1999||02:11 PM||||I can't belive no one has mentioned the safety factor!  The fiero got a 5 star safety rating in '84, beat only by a Volvo.  They have very stiff layered steel space frames, with carefully designed crumple zones, and are built like a roll cage on the top.  Go to the nearest junk yard, and take a look at the cars.  Even in those horrendous front-end-meets-telephone-pole crashes, the passenger compartment is mostly untouched.  Now, take a look at something like a CRX in a junkyard.....not a pretty sight.  ||||reg||2
Z||000008||Cooter||11-19-1999||07:49 PM||||I have had more than my share of cars over the past 15 years, and other than my Civic, the Fiero has required about the same amount of work as other cars that were much newer. The worst car that I had on eating money was my 84 Z-28 that was only 4 years old when I had it. It was an every weekend project to fix something on that thing.||||reg||1
Z||000009||RFMike||11-19-1999||08:24 PM||||I have commuted 30,000 miles or more for the past 20 years, and have usually bought used vehicles to do it in.  I have bought most of them from private parties.  If you can't tell if the car is mechanically sound, bring someone with who can.  I have always bought cars that were well maintained, even with high miles.  If the owner doesn't want to sell it, has taken care of it, and you like it, buy it.<BR>Case in point- when I was commuting to school, I needed cheap transportation.  I found a Chevette with 120K on it.  The owner had a written record of EVERY cent he had ever spent on it, and what it was for.  This was his baby (God only knows why).  I talked him down to $800, put 85K more on it, and sold it to my nephew for $50 bucks cause he needed a car for work.  <BR>Be objective.  Don't let your emotions run away with you.  <BR>Case in point- I bought a 1983 S10 Blazer from a 22 year old.  The body was in great shape, the interior was perfect, bluebook was $7800, and I got the price to $6200.  It had a noisy CV joint, but I bought it anyway.  Worst purchase I ever made.  Three trannies, one clutch, alternator, and on and on.  I didn't talk to any mechanic friends, or they would have told me that every 5speed 83 Blazer went belly up.<BR>You can find a Fiero that has been taken care of, but you'll probably have to be patient.<BR>Good luck.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Gokart||11-19-1999||08:37 PM||||I was a bit worried when I bought my GT last April that it might need alot of repair work like the 85 2M4 I had for a couple of years back in 94-96. So far (knock on wood) my repairs have been minimal. So I guess it depends alot on how well the previous owner treated the car. I think the 2M4 had been beat on and neglected!! It was a money pit! Learning to work on your own car is great advice and has saved me alot of $$$! Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 11-19-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||11-19-1999||11:40 PM||||I watched the classifieds for almost 8 months before I found a Fiero that I wanted and could afford.  Just when I was thinking "maybe next year", there was a Formula 5-speed for sale CHEAP! Car hasn't had a cent in repairs after 13,000 miles.  Just oil, filters, fresh plugs, wires, cap, rotor, regular "tune-up" stuff.  <BR>The clutch has 152K on it but it's still good. I figure, I'm off for the month, I don't want it going out A:during winter, B: in the middle of a semester, and C: when I can't get to it for a while so do it now.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Greg Piet||11-20-1999||12:27 AM||||OK this post is getting my feathers in a ruffle!<P>This is a board of fierophiles, and we will all have a biased answer!<BR>if anyone wants  a 2 seater, then of course a fiero is a good car... if not good, then it is  great!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||RFMike||11-20-1999||01:20 PM||||I disagree, Greg.  My post maybe wasn't clear.  If the car has been beat on and not maintained, it's not a good car, no matter what it is, unless you have unlimited time and/or money.  If you have limited amounts of both, you will quickly sour on any car that keeps sucking it out of you.  My .02.  No offense meant.<BR>That said, yea, a Fiero that purrs is the best kitten in the world.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000014||Monkeyman||11-22-1999||12:58 AM||||Doesn't matter if the car was beat on or not maintained..it's still a Fiero!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!  I just bought my car.  It's an '86 2m4.  Almost no options, tranny leaks, motor won't idle, etc.  This was the best I could afford.  It may take my a couple of years and a few thousand bucks, but I WILL have a killer car.  If nothing else, they don't make Fieros anymore.  Someday, even the most ragged out Fiero will be worth some money.||||reg||1
